import '../tables/decks.drift';
import '../tables/cards.drift';
import '../tables/study.drift';

-- The workload a daily reminder is allowed to talk about (BR-184, BR-188).
--
-- **One row per root deck, and a card belongs to exactly one of them.** The
-- grouping goes through `decks.root_deck_id` (BR-57), so a card is counted once
-- no matter how deep it sits and no matter how many of its ancestors are also
-- "decks with due cards". Grouping by the card's own deck would count the same
-- card again for every level a caller then rolled up, which is the double count
-- BR-188 forbids. Coalescing the parent column with the id is banned for the
-- same reason it is banned everywhere else (BR-57): it returns the level-2 deck
-- from level 3 down.
--
-- **`learned_at IS NOT NULL` is the whole of "no new cards".** A card that has
-- not finished the learning chain is *available* to study, not *owed* — and a
-- reminder that fires every day whether or not anything is owed is a reminder
-- people turn off (BR-184, BR-142).
--
-- **Two parameters, both instants, both supplied by the caller.** `:dayStart`
-- is the start of the current local day resolved from `clockProvider` and
-- `utcOffsetProvider` (BR-105); `:now` is the moment the reminder is being
-- evaluated. Nothing here reads the SQL clock — "due exactly at now" is a
-- boundary that has to be testable, and a query that reads its own clock cannot
-- be tested at one.
--
-- **The ordering here is stability, not urgency.** BR-187's ranking needs the
-- overdue *age* in local day boundaries, which is calendar arithmetic this
-- schema has no business doing; the domain sorts. `root.id ASC` only makes the
-- input order reproducible so the sort has a deterministic starting point.
reminderWorkloadPerRootDeck:
SELECT
  root.id AS rootDeckId,
  root.name AS rootDeckName,
  SUM(CASE WHEN s.due_at < :dayStart THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS overdueCount,
  SUM(CASE WHEN s.due_at >= :dayStart THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS dueTodayCount,
  MIN(CASE WHEN s.due_at < :dayStart THEN s.due_at END)
    AS oldestOverdueAt
FROM cards c
INNER JOIN decks d ON d.id = c.deck_id
INNER JOIN decks root ON root.id = d.root_deck_id
INNER JOIN card_study_states s ON s.card_id = c.id
WHERE s.learned_at IS NOT NULL
  AND s.due_at <= :now
GROUP BY root.id, root.name
ORDER BY root.id ASC;

-- The user's reminder choice, written as one statement (BR-182, BR-183).
--
-- **Both columns together.** "On" and "at this time" are one decision, and two
-- statements let a failure land between the halves — a reminder that is on at a
-- time nobody picked.
--
-- `WHERE id = 1` rather than an unfiltered UPDATE: `app_settings` holds exactly
-- one row by construction, and naming it is what keeps this statement correct if
-- that ever stops being true.
saveReminderSettings:
UPDATE app_settings
SET reminder_enabled = :enabled,
    reminder_minute_of_day = :minuteOfDay,
    updated_at = :updatedAt
WHERE id = 1;
